		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=2926&amp;p=30023#p30023"><![CDATA[
<blockquote class="uncited"><div>Hello Kris, I confirm the same behavior on my side after I saw your video. Same settings, personal mmdvm in duplex mode, manual configuration of TS filters on OFF, and briefly switching channels and returning makes the audio to come back ok. Because i lose lots of communication when I am in in a qso, I sometimes cannot hear if a topic is addressed to me or not, so for now I came back on the original FW from TYT 390. Awaiting a bugfix. Respect for the developers, their work is massively appreciated. Best regards, PA2AP, Giani</div></blockquote>Its almost impossible to fix a problem which does not occur for any of the developers.<br><br>Possibly some other calibration setting which the official firmware is applying to the hardware, which is only applied to some radios, i.e. We send a lot of unknown data to the DMR chip and also to the RF chip.<br><br>There are many undocumented parts of both the DMR and RF chip, so possibly the data that we recoded on our radios, is not correct for your radio.<br><br>The only way to confirm this would be for you to dissassemble your radio and solder wires inside and then capture all the traffic to both the DMR chip and also the RF chip, but this is very difficult and would potentially damage your radio.<br><br>I almost destroyed my MD2017 when solding wires to the CPU / MCU chip to capture the data to the RF chip, becuase I shorted multiple pins<br>Luckily I saw the short before applying power to the radio and was able to remove the solder using brade wick.<br><br>Others have not been so lucky, at least one person may have destroyed their radio by shorting the internal electronics while trying to attach a GPS.<br>I still don't know whether they managed to fix that radio<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=50">VK3KYY</a> — Fri Mar 08, 2024 12:07 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
